## Server and client

Every interface has exactly two roles.

A **server** is the *provider* of the interface. It {{ server_roles[:-1]|join(', ') }}{% if server_roles|length > 2 %},{% endif %}{% if server_roles|length > 1 %} and {% endif %}{{ server_roles[-1] }}. There may be more than one
server instance on a broker at once; each one has its own instance id.

A **client** is the *user* of the interface. It {{ client_roles[:-1]|join(', ') }}{% if client_roles|length > 2 %},{% endif %}{% if client_roles|length > 1 %} and {% endif %}{{ client_roles[-1] }}. A client first *discovers* which
server instances are on the broker, then binds to one.

```text
   ┌──────────────────────┐                       ┌──────────────────────┐
   │  {{"%-20.20s"|format(stinger.rust.client_struct_name)}}│                       │  {{"%-20.20s"|format(stinger.rust.server_struct_name)}}│
   │   (client feature)   │                       │   (server feature)   │
   └───────────┬──────────┘                       └──────────┬───────────┘
               │                                             │
               │  publish / subscribe   publish / subscribe  │
               │        ┌────────────────────────┐           │
               └───────►│       MQTT broker      │◄──────────┘
                        └────────────────────────┘
```

Because the broker sits in the middle, neither side needs the other to be running to
start up, and a client can be restarted without the server noticing.

## The four patterns

An interface is built out of four kinds of parts, which differ in which way they travel
and whether a reply comes back:

| Pattern | Direction | Reply | In this interface |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Signal** | server → client | none | {{stinger.signals|length}} |
| **Command** | client → server | none | {{stinger.commands|length}} |
| **Method** | client → server | response | {{stinger.methods|length}} |
| **Property** | server owns it; clients read it and request changes | update response | {{stinger.properties|length}} |

A command is the mirror image of a signal — the same fire-and-forget message travelling
the other way — or equivalently a method with no response. Because there is no response
channel, a command that cannot be deserialized is logged and dropped, and the server
never learns which client sent it.

